==English==

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|drive|way}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|US}} A short [[private]] [[road]] that leads to a [[house]] or [[garage]].
# {{lb|en|US}} A road that [[enter]]s or [[circulate]]s through a defined area, such as an [[institution]], [[compound]], land area, etc., for the purpose of private access, maintenance, or security.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Asphalt Answers|author=Joseph Truni|work=w:Popular Mechanics|date=April 1988|page=127|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZuQDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA127|passage=The design originally called for a 1-lane-wide '''driveway''' leading from the street to the parking area.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Catch 44|author=Franco La Monica|publisher=Christian Faith Publishing|location=Meadville, Pennsylvania|date=2020-10-28|page=37|isbn=9781098050788|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aJQuEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT37|passage=They drove in the '''driveway''' into the parking lot that was totally full.}}
# {{senseid|en|droveway}} {{lb|en|uncommon}} {{synonym of|en|droveway}}
#* {{quote-book |en |last=Rose |first=Robin |year=1991 |chapter=Appendix C: Resource Assessment: North Powder National Wild and Scenic River. USDA, Forest Service, Wallowa-Whitman National Forest |title=Environmental Assessment for the North Powder Wild and Scenic River Management Plan |publisher=US Department of Agriculture |page=12 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qZg2AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA4-PA12&lpg=RA4-PA12#v=onepage&q&f=false |passage=The river corridor had also been used as a '''driveway''' for sheep in the past. Prior to the gold rush era, it is likely that early day trappers traveled the corridor.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |author=Outdoors Southwest |year=2022 |title=Journey through New Mexico |journal=Outdoors Southwest |url=https://www.outdoorssw.com/2022-new-mexico-past-and-future |accessdate=2023-09-13 |passage=Magdalena became a major cattle town, securing one of the largest stockyard shipping pens in the Southwest beginning in 1885. "The Magdalena Trail" was a cowboy's destination driving and herding cattle and sheep to the stockyards. So prominent was this trail that it was officially claimed as a "'''driveway''' for cattle" by the Grazing Homestead Act of 1916. Today in Magdalena the wooden stockyards, worn by weather and time, still stand as a testament to the Old West Cattle Drives.}}

